// THE MAUSOLEUM AT HALICARNASSUS as it stood whole and new about 340 BC, in the afternoon of the last day of summer.
// We come in over the harbour 3 m above the water, 170 m off the quay, heading north up the fairway to the landing:
// the town rising like a theatre, the banded marble tomb on its long blue wall above it, the city wall on the crests
// behind; over the quay and the market, up the climbing street of shops, a left turn over the upper square, up the
// broad stair and through the gate's covered way, which stands far south in the east wall; then dead straight west on the
// gate's axis over the open gravel, the whole tomb on our right across the terrace; past its west end a climbing
// turn to the right; then one straight rising line east over the west end, the lions and the roof's steps, past the
// four marble horses and the empty chariot, to the portal.
// World axes: +x east, +z south, y up; the sea at y 0; the tomb's centre at x 0, z 0; the terrace at y 20.
// NOTHING HAPPENS BECAUSE WE COME: ships, boats, people, mules, birds, cloth, leaves and smoke go by the world clock
// on rounds and in boxes laid by place; the hour is one standing state per visit; the tomb and its statues never move.
// DECLARED PACING: none (no craft, companion or team goes with the visitor; the flight's own pace is the part's flightPlan,
// laid between places of the route).
// WHAT READS THE EYE (THE ONE LIST: the part and the report give it by these numbers). Each is OPTICS, a law of
// seeing, and no habit of any body; nothing else in the world or the part reads the eye's place, its direction, the
// lens or the picture's size:
//   1. THE KIT'S OWN: the camera is handed to the kit's sky, sea, birds and smoke columns and to nothing of the
//      world's own: the dome and the sea's sheet lie centred on the eye, the sea's sheen and its far fade go by the
//      kit's law, the kit's sprites turn to the eye.
//   2. THE PAINTED SKY'S SHELL lies centred on the eye (a sky has no place of its own).
//   3. THE MIRROR IMAGES ON THE WATER lie where the eye sees them (a reflection has no place of its own); they are
//      held for the warship, the merchantmen at anchor, lightering and alongside, the small boats, the quay's face
//      and the waterfront's first row of houses, and for nothing else.
//   4. THE FINE WAVELETS' TWO FACES on the water (one turned to the eye, one away).
//   5. THE LIT AIR between the eye and a thing (the part's mAir: by how much air lies between and by the look's
//      bearing from the sun's), and with it THE PEOPLE'S SHADOWS, which go with the same haze by their true distance
//      from the eye.
//   6. THE LIT SURFACE'S DIRECTION TO THE EYE (the part's one material): the sheen of marble and polished plaster,
//      the glint of bronze, the tone of carved stone toward its turning edge, which face of a thin sheet is seen,
//      and the shares of tread and riser in the broad stair's far form. P1, TWO MORE, BOTH MIRRORS: STILL WATER IN
//      A VESSEL gives back the low sky on the far side of the look (the more the flatter the look lies on it); THE
//      BRONZE ON THE GATE'S LEAVES, in the passage's shade, gives back the bright west mouth where the look's
//      mirrored ray goes out through it.
//   7. THE PIXEL'S SIZE ON A SURFACE: what is too fine for the picture goes over to ITS MEAN, never to nothing (the
//      sea's wavelets and ripples, the sky's combed plumes, courses and joints, tiles, paving, the gravel's
//      grain and raked relief, the stair's steps, flutes and small carved ornament; P1: the frames and fillets of
//      the gate's coffers, the slabs and joints of the way of slabs, the ripple on still water, and the stir of
//      the sun's flecks under a tree, which is seen near only). NOT TO ITS OWN MEAN: THE BRICK IN THE PLASTER'S
//      BARE PATCHES (P1) is seen near only; far off a bare patch keeps live's patch tone (the plaster a tenth to
//      a sixth darker), which is paler than the brick's own mean, so a patch darkens and reddens as the eye
//      nears. A THIN DARK MARK WHOSE MEAN IS
//      THE SURFACE'S OWN TONE TO WITHIN A FIFTIETH goes over to that tone (the cart ruts, the roof beams' ends, the
//      dirt line at a wall's foot, the foot marks in the gravel): said beside each in the part.
//      P1C, IN THE SKY'S HEAPED CLOUDS, by the pixel's breadth on the dome alone: A HEAD FINER THAN THE PICTURE
//      IS LIT AS THE FORM IT SITS ON (a lesser head as its great head, a great head as its heap's own round; each
//      keeps its outline), and the heaps' fray goes over to its mean. P1F (the owner's yes written 10:57 UTC on
//      12 Oct 2026): AND A GREAT HEAD FINER THAN THE PICTURE GETS NO RIM OF ITS OWN TOWARD THE SUN: its share of
//      the rim goes over to its heap's own round, by the same figure of the pixel's breadth. Lawful by the
//      owner's answer written 23:18 UTC on 11 Oct 2026, being named here.
